{"id":"h164","run":2,"role":"calibration","category":"science-chemistry","question":"Nitrous acid, HNO2, has a pKa value of 3.3. If a solution of nitrous acid is found to have a pH of 4.2, what can be said about the concentration of the conjugate acid/base pair found in solution?\nIs the correct answer \"[NO2-] > [HNO2]\"?","truth":"yes","model_answer":"yes","correct":true,"source":"TIGER-Lab/MMLU-Pro, test split","claims":[{"text":"At pH 4.2 with pKa 3.3, [NO2-] is greater than [HNO2].","confidence":0.97,"lean":1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":0.0},{"text":"When pH exceeds pKa, the deprotonated form dominates.","confidence":0.95,"lean":1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":0.0},{"text":"Henderson–Hasselbalch gives log([NO2-]/[HNO2]) = 0.9.","confidence":0.97,"lean":1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The ratio [NO2-]/[HNO2] is about 7.9, so the base is roughly eight times more abundant than the acid.","confidence":0.96,"lean":1,"theta":1.508,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The exact Ka expression gives the ratio as Ka/[H+] = 10^0.9 without relying on the buffer approximation.","confidence":0.95,"lean":1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":-0.0},{"text":"Henderson–Hasselbalch is an approximation that works best when both species are substantial and [H+] is small compared with them.","confidence":0.8,"lean":0.0,"theta":1.2566,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"[H+] is about 6.3e-5 M, so the approximation holds for any reasonable concentration.","confidence":0.85,"lean":1,"theta":1.3352,"phi":-0.0},{"text":"Nothing in the problem suggests a competing effect that would reverse the ratio.","confidence":0.85,"lean":1,"theta":1.3352,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The caveat that the approximation might fail is dismissed as irrelevant because the exact calculation confirms the result.","confidence":0.9,"lean":1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":-0.0}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":1,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":2,"J":0.8,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":3,"J":0.9,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":4,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":7,"J":0.3,